      "writtenOn": "2026-08-19T08:06:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Given that `pool_cache_is_free_index()` is required, could we encode the state directly in `next_free[]` instead of maintaining a parallel `slot_state[]` array? For example, reserve a second non-encodable sentinel for `ALLOCATED`; free entries contain their next index (including the existing end-of-list sentinel), while allocated entries contain `ALLOCATED`.\n\n`is_free_index()` would then be a relaxed atomic load of `next_free[index] !\u003d ALLOCATED`. A put could relaxed-store the local-list head into `next_free[index]`, combining the state transition and list link in one write. Double-free detection could use a debug-only CAS from `ALLOCATED`, or retain that CAS in production if hard rejection is required.\n\nThis would remove the separate state allocation and avoid touching an additional metadata cache line on every get/put. All `next_free` accesses would need relaxed atomic operations because `is_free_index()` may read them concurrently, but those compile to ordinary loads/stores. Does that fit the required `is_free_index()` semantics?",
      "parentUuid": "15c1dd17_1d7215e3",
      "revId": "758913d6c303ce23cd7c8133bd606025baef705c",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
